storage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=384&amp;q=75 384w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=640&amp;q=75 640w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=750&amp;q=75 750w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=828&amp;q=75 828w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=1080&amp;q=75 1080w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=1200&amp;q=75 1200w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=1920&amp;q=75 1920w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=2048&amp;q=75 2048w, /_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75 3840w\" src=\"/_next/image?url=https%3A%2F%2Fiiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co%2Fstorage%2Fv1%2Fobject%2Fpublic%2Ftirtha-images%2Fimg_14318.jpeg&amp;w=3840&amp;q=75\"/></div></div><article class=\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\"><p>108 Parshwanath series, No. 91 - Shri Kalpadrum Parshwanath.</p><p>Main Deity: A tall, white-hued idol of Lord Shri Kalpadrum Parshwanath, 23 inches tall and 21 inches wide, in the padmasana posture. Above the idol&#x27;s head is a canopy of seven serpent hoods.</p><p>Location: This temple is in the city of Mathura.</p><p>History: The ancient name of Mathura was Indrapur. From the time of the 7th Tirthankara, Shri Suparshwanath, Mathura became a renowned Jain pilgrimage site. A golden stupa of Shri Suparshwanath was built here by the gods. By the time of the 23rd Tirthankara, Shri Parshwanath, this golden stupa had been encased in bricks. In the 8th century, on the advice of Acharya Shri Bappabhattisurishwarji, this stupa was renovated. At this stupa, Acharya Shri Haribhadrasuriji fasted for 15 days without food and revised the &quot;Mahanishith Sutra,&quot; which had been damaged by insects. Mathura was a very famous city, full of pride and glory owing to the many historic events that took place here. It was the birthplace of Shri Krishna Vasudev. On the advice of Acharya Shri Bappabhattisurishwarji, the king of Amer had the temple at Mathura renovated in V.S. 826. A new temple was built by King Aamraja, and an idol of Lord Mahavir was consecrated in it by the revered hands of Acharya Shri Bappabhattisurishwarji. This is also the site of the nirvana (liberation) of the last Kevali, Shri Jambuswami. In the 14th century, there stood here the Shri Kalpadrum Parshwanath Temple, the Shri Neminath Bhagwan Temple, and the stupa of Mahalakshmi. It is believed that the temple of Kalpadrum Parshwanath was originally built by the courtesan Kuberasena and was later rebuilt by King Aamraja. During the Muslim invasions, at the time of Muhammad Ghori&#x27;s attack, this temple was destroyed; at that time the idol was hidden underground for its protection. Years later, it was recovered through excavation. Its final renovation (jinoddhar) was carried out in V.S. 1984 by Muni Shri Darshanvijayji. Kalpadrum Parshwanath is also known as Mathura Parshwanath. At present, this temple is managed by the Shri Chintamani Parshwanath Jain Shwetambar Pedhi, Agra.</p><p>Other Temples: At present, there is no other temple here.</p><p>Art and Sculptural Work: This is a very ancient temple. The idol of Lord Parshwanath is highly attractive, powerful, and miraculous. It is believed that if worshipped with great faith, all the wishes of devotees are fulfilled.</p><p>Directions: This temple is 4 kilometers from Mathura railway station. Agra is 54 kilometers from Mathura.</p><p>Scripture References: The Mathura pilgrimage site is mentioned in many ancient scriptures, such as the &quot;Uttaradhyayan Sutra,&quot; the &quot;Vrihat Kalpasutra Tika,&quot; the &quot;Shri Acharang Churni,&quot; the &quot;Vishrut Tirth Kalp,&quot; and the &quot;Hir Saubhagya,&quot; among others; Kalpadrum Parshwanath is mentioned in all of these. He is also mentioned in &quot;Tirthmala,&quot; &quot;365 Shri Parshvajin Nammala,&quot; &quot;Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,&quot; and &quot;Shri Bhagwan Parshwanath Stavan.&quot; An image of Kalpadrum Parshwanath is present at the Shri Vasupujya Swami Temple in Mulund.</p><p>Hymn of Praise: Without asking, without seeking, you grant devotion of your own accord; you surpass even the Kalpadrum (wish-fulfilling tree), O Lord Kalpadrum Parshwa; O sweet Lord of Mathura, churn away and destroy my delusion (moha). &quot;With loving devotion I bow to Shri Kalpadrum Parshwa.&quot;</p><p>Chanting Mantra: &quot;Om Hrim Arham Shri Kalpadrum Parshwanathaya Namah&quot;</p><p>Trust: Shri Kalpadrum Parshwanath Jain Shwetambar Temple, 792, Ghiya Mandi, Mathura - 281001, State: Uttar Pradesh, India. Phone: 0562-2503356, Mobile: 9756174977.</p><p>History of the 108 Lords Parshwanath and How to Reach Them - Part 19: With this, the pilgrimage to a total of 91 Lords Parshwanath is complete. Next, having completed the pilgrimage to 27 of the 28 forms of Parshwanath found in Rajasthan among the 108 Parshwanath, we will now together undertake the pilgrimage to the remaining one Parshwanath in Rajasthan, Shri Nageshwar Parshwanath, and to the forms of Parshwanath found in Madhya Pradesh. From Mathura and New Delhi, the Swaraj Express and the New Delhi-Indore Express trains are available to Indore. For this, one must get off at Vikramgarh Alot or Bhawanimandi. From Bhawanimandi, Kukdeshwar is 107 km; from Kukdeshwar, Vahi is 57 km; from Vahi, Nageshwar is 88 km; from Nageshwar, Unhel is 70 km; from Unhel, Ujjain is 35 km; from Ujjain, Hasampura is 12 km; and from Hasampura, Makshi is 53 km. In Kukdeshwar is No. 92, Shri Kukdeshwar Parshwanath; in Vahi is No. 93, Shri Vahi Parshwanath; in Nageshwar (Unhel, Rajasthan) is No. 94, Shri Nageshwar Parshwanath; in Unhel (M.P.) is No. 95, Shri Kamitpuran Parshwanath; in Ujjain is No. 96, Shri Avanti Parshwanath; in Hasampura is No. 97, Shri Alaukik Parshwanath; and in Makshi is No. 98, Shri Makshi Parshwanath.</p></article><nav class=\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 pt-6\"><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-111\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-left h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m15 18-6-6 6-6\"></path></svg><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Ravan Parshwanath</span></a><a class=\"group flex items-center gap-1.5 text-right font-ui text-sm text-ink-soft hover:text-maroon transition-colors\" href=\"/en/tirtha/tirtha-113\"><span class=\"max-w-[10rem] truncate sm:max-w-xs\">Shri Kukdeshwar Parshwanath</span><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" class=\"lucide lucide-chevron-right h-4 w-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><path d=\"m9 18 6-6-6-6\"></path></svg></a></nav></div></main><footer class=\"mt-auto border-t border-sandstone-deep/60 py-8\"><div class=\"mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 font-ui text-xs text-ink-soft flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-2 sm:items-center sm:j